Microchip ATFCD52 Fast Capacitive Digitizer - Linux driver
==========================================================

This repository contains the Linux driver for the Microchip ATFCD52 fast
capacitive digitizer tested on the SAMA7D65 Curiosity board.

The FCD device has two interfaces and is supported by separate modules;

  fcd52-core.ko        Core: Object-Based Protocol (OBP) support, the
                       configuration, firmware update paths, and the
                       sysfs interface.
  fcd52-i2c.ko         I2C control interface and binds to the device tree node
                       "microchip,atfcd52".
  fcd52-frame-data.ko  SPI frame data interface. The digitizer is the SPI
                       host and the SAMA7D65 FLEXCOM SPI block is the client.
                       Binds to "microchip,atfcd52-frame-data".

1. Files
--------

  Files should be copied to its own folder "fcd52" in the /drivers/iio/adc/
  directory.
  
  fcd52-core-main.c   OBP core, sysfs, update paths
  fcd52-cfg.c/.h      OBP_RAW configuration file parser
  fcd52-i2c.c         I2C transport and probe (fcd52-i2c.ko)
  fcd52-spi.c         SPI frame data driver (fcd52-frame-data.ko)
  fcd52.h             Shared definitions
  Kconfig, Makefile   Build configuration

Related files outside this directory:

  In the Documentation/devicetree/bindings/iio/adc/ directory:
     
	microchip,atfcd52.yaml
	microchip,atfcd52.example.dts
	microchip,atfcd52-frame-data.yaml
	microchip,atfcd52.frame-data.example.dts
	 
  In the dt-overlay-at91 repository:
  
    sama7d65_curiosity/sama7d65_curiosity_atfcd52.dtso
	sama7d65_curiosity/sama7d65_curiosity_spi.dtso
	sama7d65_curiosity.its      (FIT image configurations)
	 
  In the fcd52/tools directory:
	tools/fcd52_drain.c         (buffer test program)


2. Configuration options
------------------------

The options are in the Linux configuration under

  Device Drivers -> Industrial I/O -> Analog to digital converters
      -> Microchip ATFCD52 Fast Capacitive Digitizer (I2C control)
      -> Microchip ATFCD52 SPI frame data interface

  CONFIG_FCD52            (selected automatically; the core)
  CONFIG_FCD52_I2C        I2C control interface. Depends on I2C.
  CONFIG_FCD52_FRAME_DATA SPI frame data interface. Depends on OF and
                          DMADEVICES. Selects IIO_BUFFER and IIO_KFIFO_BUF.

For a module build, set both as "M":

  CONFIG_FCD52=m
  CONFIG_FCD52_I2C=m
  CONFIG_FCD52_FRAME_DATA=m

The SAMA7D65 defconfig already enables DMA and IIO. Check that
CONFIG_OF=y and CONFIG_DMADEVICES=y are set in the "sama7_defconfig" file.

Driver-level settings are taken from the device tree, not the kernel
configuration:

  microchip,cfg-name   Configuration file loaded by update_cfg. Default
                       "fcd52.cfg", looked up with request_firmware() in
                       /lib/firmware (or the firmware search path).

The firmware image used by the update_fw() is defaulted to "fcd52.fw".
The file must be a plain binary image. Convert the file using:

  xxd -r -p fcd52_10AA.enc > fcd52.fw


3. Building
-----------

The driver is built as part of the kernel tree. It needs the SAMA7D65 ARM
toolchain, the same one the kernel was configured with.

  cd fcd52_digitizer
  make ARCH=arm CROSS_COMPILE=arm-none-linux-gnueabihf- \
       M=drivers/iio/adc/fcd52 modules

This builds fcd52-core.ko, fcd52-i2c.ko and fcd52-frame-data.ko in
drivers/iio/adc/fcd52/. Copy them to the board (for example with scp) to
load them for test.

To build the device tree overlays, point KERNEL_DIR at the kernel tree or
create a an environmental variable.
  
  export KERNEL_DIR=/path/to/fcd52_digitizer
  export KERNEL_BUILD_DIR=/path/to/fcd52_digitizer

  cd dt-overlay-at91
  make sama7d65_curiosity_dtbos

To build the boot image (FIT) that contains the overlays, copy zImage and
the base DTB into the dt-overlay-at91 directory, then:

  make sama7d65_curiosity.itb

The FIT references ./zImage and ./at91-sama7d65_curiosity.dtb by relative
path.
  
  More information can be found below regarding the device tree overlay files.
  
  https://github.com/atmel-maxtouch/dt-overlay-at91

The test program is built the same way for the board:

  arm-none-linux-gnueabihf-gcc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o fcd52_drain fcd52_drain.c


4. Device tree and overlays
---------------------------

4.1 I2C control node (sama7d65_curiosity_atfcd52.dtso)

  digitizer@4b {
      compatible = "microchip,atfcd52";
      reg = <0x4b>;
      interrupts = <PIN_PA20 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;   /* CHG */
      vdd-supply = ...;
      avdd-supply = ...;
	  microchip,cfg-name = "fcd52.cfg";
  };

The CHG line is active low and must use IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW. The reg value
is the application address; the bootloader address is derived from it.

  Available address are:
      Reg value: 0x4A-0x52.

4.2 SPI frame data node (sama7d65_curiosity_spi.dtso)

  &flx4 {
      atfcd52_frame: atfcd52-frame-data@400 {
          compatible = "microchip,atfcd52-frame-data";
          reg = <0x400 0x200>;               /* SPI block inside FLEXCOM4 */
          clocks = <&pmc PMC_TYPE_PERIPHERAL 38>;
          dmas = <&dma0 AT91_XDMAC_DT_PERID(13)>;   /* FLEXCOM4 RX */
          dma-names = "rx";
          pinctrl-names = "default";
          pinctrl-0 = <&pinctrl_spi4_test>;
          microchip,control = <&atfcd52>;    /* see note below */
          status = "okay";
      };
  };

The generic spi4 node is disabled. The frame data driver owns the block
directly, because the digitizer is the SPI host and the kernel SPI core
cannot run a block in client mode.

Pin assignment for the frame data interface (FLEXCOM4):

  PA15  SS    (NPCS0, client select from the digitizer)
  PA16  SCK   (SPCK, driven by the digitizer)
  PA17  MISO  (not used by the digitizer; leave unconnected)
  PA18  MOSI  (digitizer data into the SoC)
  
  The SAMA7D65 Curiosity board's miktroBUS2 J26 SPI header was used for this test.
  The CS_PA15 is supplied from the miktroBUS1 header,
  as this pin is configured an a SS INPUT in SPI client mode.

The overlay's pinmux (pinctrl_spi4_test) must be attached to the frame data
node. If it is not, the pins stay GPIO inputs and the block never sees SS.

Note: microchip,control is a phandle, so the frame data overlay can only
reference the I2C node when both overlays are applied in the same FIT
configuration. The atfcd52 overlay's label (atfcd52) must be visible.

4.3 Boot configurations (FIT)

U-Boot selects an overlay set with "bootm <addr>#<config>". The configurations
in sama7d65_curiosity.its are:

  atfcd52      I2C control interface only
  spi          SPI overlay only
  atfcd52_eth  Base board with the I2C digitizer, the SPI overlay and the
               LAN8840 PHY (the usual boot configuration)

Use the combined configuration (atfcd52_eth) for the complete system. Note
that overlays after "#" must name configuration nodes, not feature names.

Set the boot command, for example:

  setenv bootcmd 'nand read 0x63000000 0x00180000 <size>; bootm 0x63000000#atfcd52_eth'
  saveenv

Replace <size> with the size of the image in hex. Check it with mkimage -l.


5. Installing the modules
-------------------------

Modules load in dependency order: fcd52-core, then fcd52-i2c, then
fcd52-frame-data. depmod works out the order automatically if the modules
are installed under /lib/modules.

5.1 Manual, for testing

Copy the three .ko files to the board, then load them in order:

  insmod fcd52-core.ko
  insmod fcd52-i2c.ko          # binds to the digitizer node
  insmod fcd52-frame-data.ko   # binds to the frame data node

Check the load with dmesg:

  dmesg | grep -i atfcd52

A successful I2C probe logs the family, the object table and the T700
layout. A successful frame data probe logs:

  atfcd52-frame-data ...: T700 layout 10x10, 202 bytes/frame
  atfcd52-frame-data ...: flexcom SPI client, 10x10 nodes, 202 bytes/frame

To unload, remove the modules in reverse order:

  rmmod fcd52-frame-data
  rmmod fcd52-i2c
  rmmod fcd52-core

Unbind the device before rmmod if the module is still bound:

  echo <device> > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/atfcd52-frame-data/unbind

5.2 Installed in the root filesystem

Copy the modules into the module tree and rebuild the dependency list:

  make ARCH=arm CROSS_COMPILE=arm-none-linux-gnueabihf- \
       INSTALL_MOD_PATH=/path/to/rootfs modules_install
  depmod -a                    # on the board, or with -b on the host

Then they are found by name. The device tree match (the I2C node and the
frame data node) loads each module automatically, so no explicit insmod is
needed once the overlay is applied.

5.3 Loading at boot

If modules are not autoloaded from the device tree, list them in
/etc/modules-load.d/fcd52.conf in the order below, one per line:

  fcd52-core
  fcd52-i2c
  fcd52-frame-data

systemd reads this file at boot, and modprobe loads each name along with its
dependencies.

5.4 Load order and dependencies

The modules depend on each other in two ways:

  - Symbol dependency. fcd52-i2c.ko and fcd52-frame-data.ko call functions
    exported by fcd52-core.ko, so fcd52-core must load first. modprobe
    resolves this from the installed module dependencies (depmod). insmod
    does not, so it fails with unresolved symbols if the core is missing.

  - Runtime dependency. The frame data driver reads the T700 frame layout
    through the I2C control device. If that device is not bound yet, the
    frame data probe is deferred and retried later, so the modules can load
    in either order. A deferred probe prints nothing by default. If the
    frame data device never appears, check
    /sys/kernel/debug/devices_deferred.

The drivers do not declare soft dependencies. To make the order explicit
when loading with modprobe, add this to /etc/modprobe.d/fcd52.conf:

  softdep fcd52-frame-data pre: fcd52-i2c
  softdep fcd52-i2c pre: fcd52-core

modprobe honours softdep. insmod ignores it, so when loading by hand, insert
the modules in the order in section 5.1.

To set the frame data node's status at boot, the overlay must already be in
the running device tree. Confirm with:

  ls /sys/devices/platform/soc/e2018000.flexcom/e2018400.atfcd52-frame-data/

If that directory is missing, the overlay is not applied and the frame data
module will not bind.

5.5 Firmware and configuration files

Put the files in /lib/firmware (or the firmware path set in the kernel):

  fcd52.cfg    OBP_RAW configuration file (V1 or V4). Applied by update_cfg.
  fcd52.fw     Firmware binary. Applied by update_fw.


6. Using the driver
-------------------

6.1 Control interface (I2C)

The control device is at /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-004b (bus number and
address depend on the board). Its attributes include:

  fw_version, hw_version, config_crc, drvr_version
  debug_v2_enable, debug_msg, debug_msg_lost, debug_notify
  bl_status            Bootloader status (read-only)
  update_cfg           Write "default" or a file name to apply a configuration
  update_fw            Write "fcd52.fw" (or another name) to update firmware
  mem_access           Raw binary access to the device memory map

Apply a configuration:

  echo default > /sys/bus/i2c/drivers/atfcd52/2-004b/update_cfg

Update the firmware (the device must be running or in bootloader mode):

  echo fcd52.fw > /sys/bus/i2c/drivers/atfcd52/2-004b/update_fw

The firmware update enters bootloader mode, sends the image as frames, waits
about 1 s for the application to start, re-reads the information block and
re-applies fcd52.cfg if it is present.

The update does not check the version. Every update is programmed.

6.2 Frame data interface (SPI)

The IIO device for the frame data interface is the one with a matrix_xsize
attribute:

  /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceN/

Attributes:

  matrix_xsize, matrix_ysize   Geometry from the T700 layout
  dropped_frames               Frames failing the Frame ID check
  overflow_frames              Frames rejected because the buffer was full
  dma_periods                  DMA period callbacks since capture start
  id_match_periods             Periods whose first and last byte match
  sync_locks, sync_losses      Frame sync locks and lost locks
  lag_max, lag_overruns        DMA callback lag, in periods
  last_frame                   Hex dump of the most recent raw period
  raw_capture                  Binary dump of the captured periods
  raw_capture_hex              The same periods as text, one 0xNN per line

Captured data is read through the buffer:

  /dev/iio:deviceN

Samples are 16-bit little-endian signed values, one per node, so a 10x10
matrix gives 200 bytes per scan.

6.3 Test program

tools/fcd52_drain reads the buffer and reports the frame count and loss
counters:

  ./fcd52_drain                  # 4096 scans, until Ctrl-C
  ./fcd52_drain -t 5             # stop after 5 seconds
  ./fcd52_drain -l 512 -o out.bin -d 2

Options:

  -l length   Buffer length in scans (default 4096)
  -t seconds  Stop after this many seconds
  -o file     Also write the sample data to file
  -d N        Use /dev/iio:deviceN (default: the device with matrix_xsize)


7. Logging
----------

The driver logs through dev_info() during the normal path, so the messages
show with the default console log level. Useful lines:

  atfcd52 ...: application 0x4b, bootloader 0x27
  atfcd52 ...: Family: 0xAA ... Objects: 25
  atfcd52-frame-data ...: capture started: SR=... SPIENS=1 ...
  atfcd52-frame-data ...: frame sync locked at stream position N
  atfcd52-frame-data ...: capture stopped: NSSR edges N, RDRF seen N
  atfcd52-frame-data ...: block error SR=... (OVRES ... MODF ...)

Check the boot log for the probe errors, and dmesg after each capture.


8. Status and known limits
--------------------------

I2C control interface
  Probe, configuration (update_cfg), firmware update (update_fw) and the
  bootloader-only recovery path have been exercised on the SAMA7D65 board.
  The firmware update path has been run to completion.

SPI frame data interface
  Frames are received from the digitizer. A 4 s SS/SCK/MOSI capture shows
  1355 full frames with matching IDs and a steady 2.589 ms frame period.

  The driver's frame sync is not yet reliable. Known points:

  - The receive path uses a fixed 202-byte DMA period. A frame cut short by
    the device (17 bytes was observed) shifts every later frame boundary.
    The planned fix is to frame each transaction by its SS window (NSSR).
  - The digitizer restarts its frame counter after a pause, and this has
    been seen once (frame 731 in a 4 s capture, followed by a 137 ms gap).
    The driver does not yet handle an ID discontinuity.
  - The DMA callback runs a few periods behind the engine (up to 3 periods
    observed). The ring is 16 periods to cover this margin.
  - SS glitches shorter than 16 ns appear in the analyzer captures as
    zero-length transactions. Ignore them when decoding.

  The raw capture (raw_capture, raw_capture_hex) is the best reference for
  checking the frame stream. Compare it against an analyzer capture taken at
  the same time.

Test records are kept with the driver in the working tree; the capture
files named 4s_capture.csv, 4sec_v2.csv, 4sec_v3.csv and
Reset_2_touches.csv are from the analyzer.


9. Troubleshooting
------------------

Module does not load: "Invalid module format"
  The module was built for a different target. Rebuild with ARCH=arm and
  CROSS_COMPILE set to the board's toolchain.

I2C probe fails: "application 0x4b, bootloader 0x27" followed by
  "application not answering"
  The device is not answering on its application address. If the device is
  in bootloader mode, the driver still registers update_fw. Check bl_status.

Frame data does not bind: no atfcd52-frame-data directory
  The SPI overlay is not applied. Check the FIT configuration with
  atfcd52_eth, and that the frame data node appears under flx4 in the
  device tree.

Frame data binds but no frames
  Check that the SS line is on PA15 and wired to the digitizer's SS, and that
  the pinctrl_spi4_test pinmux is attached to the frame data node. Then run
  fcd52_drain while touching the screen, and check that NSSR edges is
  non-zero in the capture stopped line.

Frame data: "cannot create duplicate filename ... dma:rx" on insmod
  The module was loaded while the previous instance was still bound. Unbind
  the device, then remove and load the module again.

update_fw fails with "bootloader did not reach WAITING_CMD"
  The device is not in bootloader mode, or it is not answering on the
  bootloader address. Check bl_status and the I2C address mapping.
